WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.931
Sending an email can involve several relays
before the destination accepts it. If the

00:00:04.931 --> 00:00:10.152
original client waits for that entire chain, a
slow or temporarily unavailable destination

00:00:10.152 --> 00:00:15.431
keeps the compose interface waiting. An
asynchronous contract lets the first service
accept

00:00:15.431 --> 00:00:18.216
responsibility and return before final delivery.

00:00:18.216 --> 00:00:23.459
The response still matters: without it, the
client cannot distinguish acceptance from a lost

00:00:23.459 --> 00:00:28.703
network request. But its meaning has changed
from completed to accepted for later work. Draw

00:00:28.703 --> 00:00:33.034
two separate milestones on the timeline, first
acceptance and then delivery.

00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:38.030
A quick response is honest only if the interface
describes the first milestone. The user

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.955
should not see a delivery guarantee when the
system has merely stored work that it intends

00:00:42.955 --> 00:00:43.557
to attempt.

00:00:43.716 --> 00:00:48.728
A report export may take several minutes, but
the user still needs the finished file or a

00:00:48.728 --> 00:00:53.910
useful failure explanation. Create an operation
record and return its identifier immediately

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:58.810
after durable acceptance. The client can revisit
that record while its state moves from

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.232
pending to running to successful or failed.

00:01:01.232 --> 00:01:06.338
A callback is another option when the receiver
has an accessible endpoint, but it needs

00:01:06.338 --> 00:01:11.502
authentication and retry handling. A browser
that closes is not automatically a reliable

00:01:11.502 --> 00:01:16.548
callback server. For a consequential operation,
hiding all failures in an operator log

00:01:16.548 --> 00:01:18.309
leaves the user unable to act.

00:01:18.309 --> 00:01:23.082
Choose the feedback path together with the
acceptance contract, and retain the result long

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:25.575
enough for an interrupted client to recover it.

00:01:25.809 --> 00:01:31.342
A user publishes a short message. The API
authenticates the session, records the request,

00:01:31.342 --> 00:01:36.876
and places a publication job on a queue. The job
contains the author, message identifier,

00:01:36.876 --> 00:01:42.596
content reference, and fields the publication
worker needs. That schema plays the role of an

00:01:42.596 --> 00:01:48.068
interface: a producer and consumer must agree on
its meaning even though they do not run

00:01:48.068 --> 00:01:49.000
simultaneously.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.563
The publication worker later finds the followers
and updates their feeds. Optional

00:01:53.563 --> 00:01:58.515
notification jobs go to another queue because
sending an alert has different dependencies

00:01:58.515 --> 00:02:03.190
and retry behavior. Drawing these stages
explains why one cheap API call can trigger

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:08.198
thousands of later operations without making the
user wait for every individual feed write

00:02:08.198 --> 00:02:09.311
and device delivery.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:14.696
Now stop a worker immediately after it adds a
post to a feed but before it acknowledges the

00:02:14.696 --> 00:02:19.549
queue message. The broker may deliver that
message again because completion was never

00:02:19.549 --> 00:02:23.659
confirmed. Without duplicate protection, the
same post can appear twice.

00:02:23.659 --> 00:02:28.558
An idempotency key lets the consumer recognize
that this logical operation has already

00:02:28.558 --> 00:02:33.683
produced its effect. A second failure window
exists at publication: updating business data

00:02:33.683 --> 00:02:38.866
and then separately enqueueing a message can
leave a committed post with no downstream work

00:02:38.866 --> 00:02:40.917
if the process crashes between them.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:45.961
A transactional outbox records the business
change and pending event together, then

00:02:45.961 --> 00:02:51.109
publishes the event later. Reliability depends
on these boundaries, not merely on calling

00:02:51.109 --> 00:02:52.151
the queue durable.
